# Groadmap project settings. Format: `key = value`, one per line.
# `#` starts a comment. Unknown keys are ignored.
#
# This file is SHARED. Both skills that drive `rmp` read it with the same logic:
#   * roadmap-manager    — reads `roadmap` only (tasks, sprints, backlog).
#   * knowledge-authority — reads `roadmap` and `socket` (the knowledge graph).
# So when either one edits this file it amends only its own key and preserves
# every other line: dropping `socket` breaks the graph connection, and dropping
# `roadmap` breaks both skills.
#
# The legacy form of this file was a single bare line holding just the roadmap
# name. Both skills still accept it; this keyed form supersedes it.

# The rmp roadmap that holds this project's tasks, sprints and knowledge graph.
# Must match ^[a-z0-9_-]+$, at most 50 characters. Passed to rmp as `-r`.
roadmap = gograph

# Unix domain socket for the knowledge-graph server. `rmp graph serve` binds it
# and `rmp graph client` connects to it, so BOTH ends must resolve the same path.
#
# This is deliberately the path rmp derives from the roadmap name. Keeping it
# means `rmp web` — which cannot be told a socket — resolves the same file and
# still works. A non-default path here makes the web interface unable to reach
# the graph.
#
# `~` is expanded to $HOME. The path must fit the platform's socket-address
# limit — measured at 103 bytes on macOS — or `serve` fails with exit 1 and
# says so.
socket = ~/.roadmaps/gograph/graph.sock
